1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:07,249
In this lesson we watch Dijkstra's algorithm find the shortest paths in a small graph, and you will predict its next move.

2
00:00:08,049 --> 00:00:26,628
Every vertex keeps a tentative distance, its best distance found so far. The source starts at zero, everything else at infinity. Relaxing an edge from u to v asks one question. Is going through u shorter than what v already has? If so, v gets the new, smaller distance.

3
00:00:27,528 --> 00:00:37,981
Here is a small directed graph on VisuAlgo. The numbers on the arrows are edge weights. We want the shortest distance from vertex zero to every other vertex.

4
00:00:42,131 --> 00:00:48,284
In the menu, Dijkstra's algorithm is open, and the source s is already zero, which is what we want.

5
00:00:50,034 --> 00:00:55,190
We start the original version, and rewind to the first frame so we can step through it slowly.

6
00:00:58,982 --> 00:01:06,670
Vertex zero, the source, has distance zero. Every other vertex shows Inf, infinity, because we have not reached it yet.

7
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:17,212
Vertex zero has the smallest distance, so we explore it first. The edge to vertex one has weight nine, so d of one becomes nine.

8
00:01:18,962 --> 00:01:27,593
The edge to vertex two gives it seventy five. Vertex zero has no more outgoing edges, so its distance, zero, is now final.

9
00:01:29,343 --> 00:01:38,785
The priority queue now holds vertex one at nine and vertex two at seventy five. Dijkstra always takes the smallest, so vertex one is next.

10
00:01:40,535 --> 00:01:48,904
The edge from one to two would give nine plus ninety five, one hundred and four. That is worse than seventy five, so nothing changes.

11
00:01:50,654 --> 00:01:56,557
The edge from one to four gives nine plus forty two, fifty one. Vertex one is now final.

12
00:01:58,307 --> 00:02:07,754
Vertex two has been waiting at seventy five since the very first step. Vertex four just got fifty one. Which one does Dijkstra finalize next?

13
00:02:13,654 --> 00:02:21,247
Vertex four, at fifty one. Dijkstra does not care which vertex it found first, only which one is closest right now.

14
00:02:25,397 --> 00:02:33,413
From four, the edge of weight thirty one reaches vertex three. Fifty one plus thirty one is eighty two. Vertex four is final.

15
00:02:35,163 --> 00:02:39,384
Next comes vertex two, at seventy five, the smallest distance left.

16
00:02:41,134 --> 00:02:49,607
Its edge to three would give one hundred and twenty six, worse than eighty two, so vertex three keeps its distance. Vertex two is final.

17
00:02:51,357 --> 00:02:55,877
Last is vertex three. Its edge back to vertex one cannot beat nine.

18
00:02:57,967 --> 00:03:03,625
Every vertex is now final, and the red edges form the shortest path tree from vertex zero.

19
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:17,985
The final distances are zero, nine, seventy five, eighty two and fifty one. Notice that the best route to three uses three edges, through one and four, not the two edge route through two.

20
00:03:19,635 --> 00:03:32,217
To recap. Keep a tentative distance for every vertex. Always finalize the closest unvisited vertex, then relax its edges. This works as long as no edge weight is negative.
